+# Adversarial teardown — 2026-10-12
+
+Status: accepted fixes implemented; final serial checks and judgment gate in progress. Local work only.
+
+Target: the documented Rust NBA/BAA teammate explorer, baseline `a6382963ad83f0220f6d6cb36f49faa17c657bb3`. Initial worktree had no tracked edits and two untracked user artifacts: `.jev-browser/` and `docs/reports/adversarial-teardown-2026-10-11/`. Both are preserved. This run uses its own report and artifact filenames. No source refresh, publication, deployment, commits, or external messages are part of this run.
+
+Environment: macOS arm64, Rust 1.98.1, Node 26.9.0, Python 3.14.7, existing wasm32 target and wasm-bindgen 0.2.129, installed Playwright/Chromium. Startup: `env -u OPENROUTER_API_KEY -u TYPESAFE_API_KEY NBA_PORT=3312 ./scripts/run-app.sh`. No product semantic provider calls. Configured Jev investigation tools are used separately, with bounded public source/diff excerpts and disposable inputs; no keys or private user data are sent. No new dependencies.
+
+Budget: initially 60 minutes, approximately 20 investigation, 20 implementation, 20 verification/reporting; expand only if consequential evidence warrants it. Start 2026-10-12 21:27 EDT. Actual end/elapsed time will be recorded below.
+
+Persona: simulated skeptical basketball researcher seeking Quincy Acy → Andrew Bogut and dated teammate evidence; no semantic key, keyboard route and 390×844 phone viewport. API integrator probes use a real local HTTP consumer. Personas generate hypotheses; no claim about real users' preferences or adoption follows.
+
+## Acceptance and coverage map
+
+Criteria selected before product changes; no revised criterion replaces a failing one. R02 now explicitly records the README's stable-ID promise discovered during inspection.
+
+| ID | Requirement and observable acceptance | Coverage / evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| R01 | Documented wrapper builds/starts pinned reports without credentials; browsing works and unavailable semantics is visible | Tested: baseline full receipts, E01 HTTP probes, E03 browser journey |
+| R02 | Known, typo, Unicode and alias queries retrieve correct IDs; duplicate names stay distinct and visible identity context permits choosing either; empty/nonsense/oversize input never silently selects | Tested: E04/E05 failing-before regressions, E06 whole pinned option set and visible IDs, E07 actual browser |
+| R03 | Exact direct/historical/self/disconnected paths; equal alternatives paginate without loss/duplication; invalid requests reject | Tested: E01 12 alternatives, E02 independent BFS, baseline Rust suite |
+| R04 | Dated evidence has 121 days for Acy–Bogut; appearance proofs keep unknown duration; incomplete coverage stays explicit | Tested representative outputs and baseline provenance/real-data tests; historical source truth partially tested |
+| R05 | Native keyboard/text route, phone layout, graph retention and invalid-state recovery | Partially tested: seven browser journeys, E03/E07 visible-label selection and keyboard submit; native popup arrow navigation unverified |
+| R06 | Bounded requests preserve browsing responsiveness; first statistics work and warm cache measured | Tested: E01 concurrent search, cold/warm stats; resource exhaustion/platform behavior untested |
+| R07 | Meaningful regressions fail before fixes/pass after; required full checks pass; additions have eligible execution paths | Tested: isolated full checks 129 Rust/108 Python/7 browser, credential-free start; F03 default-runner recheck pending; no execution dependencies added |
+
+The known reference set is the pinned graph plus explicitly named identities and existing fixture tests. E02 independently computes distances from the served network; that verifies algorithm agreement on this graph, not historical completeness. Retrieval precision and completeness are reported separately in the final experiments. External research breadth is bounded by the demonstrated gap; a skipped search batch is retained as a gap, not treated as corroboration.
+
+Exclusions: exhaustive NBA/BAA source reconstruction, raw-source refresh and contemporary roster freshness, paid/live semantic quality, external production deployment, screen-reader participant testing, Windows/Linux/Safari runtime testing, denial-of-service stress. These are not completion claims. Existing provider credentials and paid semantic service are baseline constraints, not eligible new additions. No shipped-binary question arose; source and live local app evidence suffice, so REA is not applicable.
