+
+## Findings and dispositions
+
+### F01 — identical connection option labels
+
+Requirement R02; type demonstrated friction; impact recoverable selection burden; evidence observed HTTP/HTML plus source and regression; confidence limited to this native select workflow. Expected: a user can distinguish same-name identities, based on README identity-selection promise. Actual: `/chain?from=brownde01` contains `<option value="brownde01" selected>Dee Brown</option>` and `<option value="brownde03">Dee Brown</option>`. 39 duplicate names cover 81 identities. Smallest reproduction: start a connection and inspect the two Dee Brown options. Values are distinct; graph identity is not merged.
+
+Strongest counterevidence: search cards already expose different eras and teams, and choosing canonical IDs through URLs/API works. Discriminating probe: inspect actual option labels and use a label-only selection; if the option itself has visible era/ID context the finding fails. Actual labels do not. Jev classifies this as friction (.88), not proven incorrect graph data. Action D01: add era and ID context only to duplicate names, with ID fallback when metadata is absent. Acceptance: all duplicate choices visibly distinct, exact preselection and unique-name labels preserved, hostile metadata escaped, native keyboard submission unchanged. Disposition: fixed and verified by E04/E06/E07; final full-check gate pending.
+
+### F02 — stable IDs hidden in links
+
+Requirement R02; type confirmed documentation/UI mismatch; impact minor identification burden; evidence source/visible card and regression. README says results show stable IDs; actual card/profile text shows only name, seasons, teams (ID exists in href/API). Smallest reproduction: search Dee Brown and read card text. Counterevidence: IDs can be recovered from URLs; this limits impact but does not fulfill visible output. Discriminating probe: text-only readback of cards/profile; presence of ID text would reject the finding. Action D02: show escaped canonical ID alongside existing context. Acceptance: result cards and profiles visibly display correct IDs. Disposition: fixed and verified by failing-before E05, passing E06/E07; final full-check gate pending.
+
+### F03 — intermittent verification timeouts with parallel browsers
+
+Requirement R07; type demonstrated verification friction; impact recoverable verification/operational burden, not a proved product navigation defect; evidence actual check logs and discriminating run. Expected: documented full runner completes every journey reliably on this configured host. Actual final parallel matrix failed three journeys on search/load-state/native evidence popup, while baseline parallel had passed and targeted identity journey passed. Smallest operational reproduction: full/browser profile's default parallel matrix in this host; intermittent, not deterministically reproduced each run. Counterevidence: baseline parallel passed, and app pages/evidence are usable in targeted and HTTP probes. Precise cause (resource scheduling, focus, load-event timing) remains unestablished. Discriminating probe changed test concurrency without code/assertion changes: one-thread full run passed all ten checks, including seven browser/canvas scenarios.
+
+Action D08: `--test-threads=1` only in runner browser-matrix argv and corresponding docs/individual command. No scenario or assertion removed; portable tests keep normal parallelism; each app server remains multithreaded. Acceptance: default documented full runner passes all ten checks after the change. Tradeoff: browser suite runs sequentially, losing parallel speed. Disposition: implemented; default verification pending. This is an operational mitigation, not proof of the underlying timeout cause or a general CI/platform reliability guarantee.
+
+### Retained/rejected proposals
+
+- P01: graph identity collapse rejected by distinct IDs and degree-three connection between the two Dee Browns; Jev contradiction confidence 1.0.
+- P02: browsing blocked by cold statistics rejected within this workload: cold 22,636.55 ms; concurrent exact search 45.71 ms; warm stats 7.25 ms. OnceLock plus blocking-task offload retained. This does not prove resistance to arbitrary request floods.
+- P03: replace all native selects with custom autocomplete: capability opportunity, not an accepted defect. W3C research and D01 favor contextual labels for the demonstrated gap. Long-list navigation remains a nonessential unmeasured opportunity.
+- H01/H02: browser observation limits and inconsistent autonomous trace/readback are harness limitations; no app defect inferred. Assisted outcome must remain explicit.
+
+## Experiments and decisions
+
+| ID | Hypothesis, control and result that would change decision | Result / disposition |
+|---|---|---|
+| X01 → D01 | Distinct IDs are indistinguishable in connection controls. Compare native labels with disambiguated search, select by label. Reject if option labels themselves distinguish eras. | E01/E03: identical labels, first ID selected by name; 39 groups/81 identities. E06 final: zero repeated labels across all 5,106 options; later ID preselected and chooseable by visible label. Implemented duplicate-only contextual labels. |
+| X02 → D02 | Stable IDs promised by README are absent from visible search/profile text. Inspect rendered text and before/after regression. Reject if ID is already visible. | E05 before fails, E07 after passes; IDs exposed without URL inspection. Implemented shared escaped context text. |
+| X03 → D03 | Exact paths or pages may omit/duplicate alternatives. Independent BFS control and whole-page versus one-item cursor replay; any mismatch changes retention decision. | All 28 BFS cases match; all 12 equal degree-two alternatives match exactly. All 101,395 edges retrieved once across 21 pages, equal to network payload; deterministic engine retained. |
+| X04 → D04 | Cold stats may block browsing or repeat costly computation. Concurrent search plus second stats request distinguishes. | 22.6-second cold calculation; concurrent 45.7-ms search; 7.25-ms warm request. Existing cache/offload retained. Single run timings, no throughput/percentile claims. |
+| X05 → D05 | Credentials/home configuration may be secretly necessary for deterministic app. Start existing binary with empty HOME and only PATH/HOME/port/asset-dir env; fail if key/service necessary. | E08 pinned app loads 5,106 players/101,395 edges, semantic unconfigured. No newly required dependency; offline browsing design retained. |
+| X06 → D06 | New label length or selection context may regress phone/browser behavior. 390×844 bounds, visible-label selection, preselection and keyboard submit; exact path must equal chosen ID. | E07 control widths fit; correct later identity and self-chain `[brownde03]`. Synthetic native-popup ArrowDown behavior remains a harness/platform coverage gap, identical before/after. No app changes justified by that probe. |
+| X07 → D07 | Rustdoc failure may reflect patch breakage or shared specialflag artifacts. Run unchanged code with normal standalone doc rebuild, then serial full checks. | E10 normal rebuild passes. Concurrent artifact explanation supported as inference; all docs checks retained, no suppressions or source fix. Serial full result appended below. |
+| X08 → D08 | Parallel browser execution may cause intermittent verification failures. Run same final assertions/code with one thread; if still failing, serialization is not a sufficient mitigation. | E11 one-thread full run passes 129 Rust/108 Python/7 browser tests; selected browser-only runner serialization. Default-runner result appended below. |
+
+Precision and completeness are separate: six positive search inputs have seven expected identity occurrences, retrieved 7/7 relevant and covered 7/7 expected, before and after. Two empty/nonsense controls return no candidate; oversize returns 400. This hand-labeled case set tests specified names/variations only. All graph-edge pages were covered, but historical **source** completeness remains unestablished. Probe invariants checked 148,802 appearance-count proofs, 1,546 dated proofs and 437 game-witness proofs; these are proof records, not disjoint edge counts. Appearance arithmetic and record structure pass, but original historical sources were not independently reconstructed. Graph statistics, proof counts, edge-page progression and the 28 distances match before/after.
+
+Tradeoff: adding context increases duplicated-name label length and builds one small name-count map per form; unique-name labels are unchanged. All 5,106 options remain available. At 390×844 the controls fit with width 313 and x=16; no measured layout regression. This does not solve unmeasured long-list discoverability or establish screen-reader compatibility.
+
+## Alternatives and eligibility
+
+Research was gap-driven, not a source-category quota. The first search batch found W3C primary form/combobox guidance and community discussion leads. A second broader NBA/combobox batch received Jev `skip` and was excluded; no competing project's mechanics/licensing are claimed from it. The chosen primary reference is the [W3C editable combobox example](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/combobox/examples/combobox-autocomplete-list/) (last updated 12 August 2025; read 12 October 2026) and [combobox pattern](https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/combobox/). The example warns that assistive-technology/mobile support needs verification; custom selection also requires keyboard/focus/state management. Those obligations matter because only native labels, not custom interaction, are broken here.
